From ed9f4229bf2ed38f859dd96bcd5e977fe92c7723 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aryan Randeriya <aryan.k.randeriya@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:21:07 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 07/18] fix(frames): resolve layout, style, scroll and script
 ops in the caller's document

Every op that takes a node id, or reads document-level layout, took the
page's document even when a frame realm called it. Inside an iframe (such as
Cloudflare's Turnstile challenge frame) a node id then named nothing, or an
unrelated node, in the page's tree:

- getBoundingClientRect, client*/offset*, getComputedStyle, element and
  root scroll metrics and scrollTo, ResizeObserver and IntersectionObserver
  measured the page's node with the same id (a frame's 123x45 div read back
  as the page's 500x40 one).
- op_script_try_start checked the page's tree, so a script inserted by frame
  code never ran; op_script_mark_started had the same bug.
- Image metadata, canvas surfaces and paint damage, WAAPI animations,
  document.fonts and the render-task clock all wrote into the page's state.

All of them now take the realm frame id and use frame_state(), as op_dom and
the shadow-root ops do; bootstrap.js passes _realmFrameId.

With layout reading the frame's own document, the frame also needs its own
viewport: FrameRealm::set_viewport now sets it on the frame's state, not only
on innerWidth/innerHeight. A frame's parser-inserted scripts are marked
already-started, as the page's are, so moving one does not run it again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tm2SNEJ42hMPiZ6cFb8Vih
